Obtaining different parameter estimates to DISTANCE can sometimes happen
as mrds and DISTANCE use different optimisation routines to maximise the
likelihood. Sometimes mrds will produce better results, sometimes
DISTANCE will. Checking plots of the detection function can show whether
or not sometime has gone disastrously wrong in the fitting procedure,
after that looking at the likelihood reported in DISTANCE and mrds will
give an idea which gives a "better" model. I am working on improving the
optimisation in mrds (and by extension Distance).

