> > Hi

> > I was looking for guidance on maximum number of knots to use for covari=
ates for GAM in Distance. I've seen different authors use different degre=
es of fitting for seabirds. As I understand it, it comes down to predicti=
ve context. If you use just the two covariates lat and lon as a multidime=
nsional smooth, by default Distance will let you have up to 30 'knots' an=
d if you accept this you'll generally attain 'better' GCV scores, and the=
 data will fit your observations better. So if you wanted a map of what y=
ou saw during survey and you were reasonably happy with your transect des=
ign and spacing, then that would be useful. However if you want a map sho=
wing what could be there if you went out and did the survey again, you're=
 better off having a lower maximum number of knots (say 6) so it won't fi=
t the model too tightly. However this will result in a visual surface whi=
ch doesn't closely match your observations. Is it better in the absence o=
f other covariates which may explain the distribution to go for the model=
 that fits the data better (e.g. more knots) but explain it as just as do=
ing that? Pooling data between surveys may then allude to more general di=
stributions where the birds like to be, but then we've introduced issues =
of temporal trends, etc... Your expert views appreciated.

